## 1. Introduction
Surface area and pore volume of porous solids has been a subject of immense interest in physical characterization [1–3]. Although it is not strictly correct, they have been extensively used as an indicator on how good an adsorbent is [4–13]. One of the popular methods frequently used for surface area determination is the BET method [5]. Although other methods can be used to obtain the surface area [14,15], this remains the method of choice because it is simple and quick, although its assumptions are not readily satisfied by all practical solids and modern advanced solids such as metal organic frameworks. Two methods are normally employed to determine the void volume: one is the helium expansion method and the other is based on adsorption of a subcritical fluid (such as argon and nitrogen) at the saturation vapour pressure. The first method usually assumes no adsorption of helium and the second method assumes that the adsorbed phase has the same density as the liquid adsorptive. Both of these present problems: for example helium adsorbs in fine pores and the assumption that the adsorbed phase behaves as liquid lacks credibility as it is well known that the adsorbed phase can be denser than the liquid phase because of the external field due to the adsorbent.The objective of this paper is to present a new method based on Monte Carlo integration to determine the accessible volume and accessible surface area, for crystalline solids with known atomic configuration. Besides these two structural parameters, this new method is used to calculate the distribution of the surface area according to the different type of solid atoms in the configuration and also the fractal dimension as a structural parameter to quantify the roughness of the surface.
The objective of this paper is to present a new method based on Monte Carlo integration to determine the accessible volume and accessible surface area, for crystalline solids with known atomic
configuration. Besides these two structural parameters, this new method is used to calculate the distribution of the surface area according to the different type of solid atoms in the configuration and also the fractal dimension as a structural parameter to quantify the roughness of the surface.
## 2. Theory
The method to determine the accessible volume is similar to that presented earlier [16]. In this paper we present a modification to that method in which we collect information about the variation of the accessible volume with distance from the surface. The derivative of the accessible volume with distance is simply the area with respect to distance. This gives us the interfacial (solid–fluid) accessible surface area, and also shows how the pore curvature changes. The interfacial (solid–fluid) accessible surface is defined as the boundary on which the solid–fluid potential energy of a fluid particle is zero. Before discussing our new methodology, we review briefly the Monte Carlo method to determine surface area that is available in the literature, and discuss its problems.
2.1. Previous Monte Carlo method to determine accessible surface area
For solids with known atomistic configuration, such as metal organic frameworks, the Monte Carlo integration method can be used to obtain the geometrical surface area which is essentially the area traced by the centre of a sphere rolling over the solid surface [17,18]. The yardstick of this measurement is the size of the
sphere. If the sphere rolls over the surface, such that the distance between the centre of this sphere and the centre of the closest solid atom is \(\sigma_{\mathrm{sf}}\) (cross collision diameter between the adsorbate molecule and the solid atom, \(\sigma_{\mathrm{sf}}=(\sigma_{\mathrm{ss}}+\sigma_{\mathrm{ff}})/2\)), this surface area is called the hard sphere surface area. A special case of this is when the size of the test sphere is zero, the resulting surface is known as the Connolly surface [19].
Let us now discuss the traditional Monte Carlo scheme to determine the geometrical surface area and point out the problem with this approach. For a given solid atom, say atom \(k\), the possible locations of the centre of a probe particle are on the surface of a sphere whose centre is the same as the centre of the solid atom "\(k\)" and the sphere radius is \(\sigma_{k}\). Thus the maximum possible geometrical surface area contributed purely by this solid atom is the area of the sphere \((4\pi\sigma_{k}^{2})\). However, not all this area is accessible to a probe particle because of the neighbouring solid atoms. To determine the accessible area we choose a point on this sphere of radius \(\sigma_{k}\) at random, place the centre of a probe particle at this point and compute its potential energy with the solid (i.e. all the solid atoms). If this is positive the selection of this point is a failure; otherwise it is a success. We repeat this process many times, and at the end of the process the fraction of successes, \(g_{k}\), is calculated. The accessible area associated with this solid atom is \(g_{k}\) \((4\pi\sigma_{k}^{2})\). We continue this process for each solid atom in sequence until we have accounted for all solid atoms. The total geometrical surface area is \(S_{\mathrm{geo}}=\sum_{k}g_{k}\left(4\pi\sigma^{2}\right)_{k}\), where \(k\) denotes the \(k\)th solid atom (the heavy red line in Fig. 1a).
That seems to be simple enough, but there is a difficulty with this approach because the accessible area with respect to the solid atom \(k\) is \(g_{k}\left(4\pi\sigma^{2}\right)_{k}\). This is the area associated purely with this \(k\)th solid atom because of the choice of the surface of the \(\sigma_{k}\)- sphere. The correct area for sampling is actually smaller because in the presence of the surrounding solid atoms the molecular probe can probe slightly deeper into the \(k\)th atom (red line Fig. 1b). Although this correction is very small, the incorrect determination of the zero potential surface can give substantial errors in the determination of surface area. This is the case for narrow pores whose size is comparable to the size of
the probe molecule. We test this with the characterization of a \(0.69\,\mathrm{nm}\) slit pore and the results show a difference of \(17\%\) between these two methods. In addition, it is worthwhile to develop a consistent generic method and this can be built-in with the process of determination of accessible volume that we will describe next. It allows us to determine the accessible volume and the accessible area in the same process, making the determination of all solid properties in a self-consistent manner. Furthermore by doing this, we can reduce the computation time for the determination of the accessible pore volume and surface area.
## 2.2. The methodology for accessible volume and accessible surface area
The accessible pore volume is defined as the volume accessible to the centre of mass of the adsorbate particle at zero loading. For solids whose atomistic configurations are known (such as crystalline solids), this can be determined with the integration Monte Carlo method [16]. For the purpose of characterization, a simple molecular probe is chosen (even though the method that we will describe works for all adsorbates). Noble gases are the best candidate for this, and argon is the logical choice because it is readily available. The choice of noble gas is appropriate for solid characterization because it is inert, spherical and non-polar. Nitrogen is also a good candidate and is the most widely used adsorptive because it is cheaper than noble gases, but in terms of adsorption it is more specific for electrostatically charged sites because it carries a quadrupole moment and therefore favours high affinity functional groups.
The Monte Carlo procedure for finding the accessible volume and accessible surface area is as follows: we insert a particle at a random position in the simulation box, which is the representative sample volume of the solid for which we would like to determine the accessible volume. After this insertion, we calculate its potential energy with the solid by summing all pairwise potential energies between this test particle and all solid atoms, and then remove it from the box to simulate the zero loading condition. If
the solid–fluid potential is zero or negative, that insertion is a success, otherwise, it is a failure. If the fluid particle has a shape (for example nitrogen or carbon dioxide), we perform this step as follows: First, we insert the centre of mass of that particle at a random position and give it a random orientation. If the solid–fluid potential energy is non-positive, we count that insertion as a success. Otherwise, we choose another orientation at random and if it becomes non-positive the insertion is a success. If after \( M \) orientations the solid–fluid potential remains positive, the insertion is a failure. The insertion procedure is repeated many times with uniform random distribution (i.e. every point within the simulation box has an equal chance of being selected, so that we sample the space of the simulation box with equal probability), and if the fraction of success is \( f \) then the accessible volume \( (V_{\mathrm{acc}}) \) is simply \( f \) times the volume of the simulation box, \( V_{\mathrm{acc}} = f V_{\mathrm{box}} \). This is the accessible volume to the centre of mass of a particle.
We have presented a Monte Carlo integration method to determine the total accessible volume. Now we take this process a step further. For each successful insertion, we determine the distance \( d \), between the centre of the successfully inserted particle and the surface of the closest solid atom. The type of this solid atom is also noted. If the distance \( d \) falls between the specified distances \( d_{i} \) and \( d_{i+1} \), and the closest solid atom has a well depth of \( \varepsilon_{i} \), we store that successful insertion in the two-dimensional volume bin \( (i, j) \) and increase the accessible volume of that bin by \( \Delta V \), where \( \Delta V \) is the volume associated with the insertion. If we carry out the insertion \( M \) times, we have \( \Delta V = V_{\mathrm{box}} / M \) (because we sample the simulation box with a uniform distribution so that every point in the box has an equal chance to be selected). Here \( V_{\mathrm{box}} \) is the volume of the simulation box. Once we have completed \( M \) insertions, the accessible volume of the bin \( (i, j) \) is \( V_{i j} \), and the total accessible volume is simply the sum of individual accessible volumes of all bins, that is:
\[
V_{\mathrm{acc}}=\sum_{i}\sum_{j}V_{i j}
\]
If we need to know the accessible volume of the region whose distance from the solid surface falls between \( d_{i} \) and \( d_{i+1} \), then this volume is obtained by summing the 2D-accessible volume over the complete spectrum of well depths of all solid atoms on the exposed surface of the solid, i.e.
\[
V_{\mathrm{acc},i}=\sum_{j}V_{i,j}
\]
Similarly, the volume of the accessible spaces above solid atoms of well depth \( (\varepsilon_{j}) \) is the sum of 2D-accessible volume over the complete spectrum of distance:
\[
V_{\mathrm{acc},j}=\sum_{i}V_{i,j}
\]
Knowing the 2D-accessible volume of the bin \( (i, j) \) for all \( i \) and \( j \), we can compute the accessible area of the bin \( (i, j) \) as follows:
\[
A_{ij}=\frac{V_{ij}}{d_{i+1}-d_i}
\]
This can be regarded as the area at the distance \( d_{i+1/2} = (d_{i} + d_{i+1})/2 \) and a molecule residing on this area would be in the surroundings of a solid atom having a well depth of \( \varepsilon_{j} \). Given this information, we can obtain the total area at the distance \( d_{i+1/2} \) as the sum of \( A_{i,j} \) over the spectrum of well depth of solid atom:
\[
A_{i}=\sum_{j}A_{ij}
\]
At distances very close to the solid atoms the local accessible volume is zero (because of the repulsion by the solid atoms). The first non-zero \( A_{i} \) is defined as the interfacial solid–fluid area that fulfils the requirement of zero solid–fluid potential energy. We can call this the contact accessible surface area or solid–fluid interfacial surface area. If we let the first non-zero \( A_{i} \) \( (i=1,2,\ldots) \) be \( A_{k} \), then the way \( A_{l} \) \( (l>k) \) behaves will give us information about the curvature of the surface. If there is no variation in \( A_{l} \), we have a flat surface. If it decreases, this indicates a concave pore, while an increase in \( A_{k} \) indicates a convex pore (see Fig. 2).
With regard to the choice of specified distances, \( \{d_{k}\} \), we choose an equal spacing \( \Delta = d_{k+1} - d_{k} \), and the procedure for obtaining the set of \( \{A_{k}\} \) can be repeated for various values of \( \Delta \). This can be done in the same process of obtaining the accessible volume. That is there is no need to repeat the MC integration for different values of \( \Delta \). Once this is done we simply plot the first non-zero \( A_{k} \) as a function of \( \Delta \). Extrapolation of this to the zero axis (i.e. \( \Delta \rightarrow 0 \)) will give a more accurate surface area of the boundary of the accessible volume.
## 2.3. The methodology for fractal dimension
Besides the accessible volume and the surface area, another important property of a solid is the roughness of the surface. That can be measured experimentally by using small-angle X-ray scattering [20], neutron scattering and adsorption measurements [21,22]. One indicator that measures the surface area roughness is the fractal dimension. There are different methods to calculate the fractal dimension of a surface [23]. Essentially the fractal dimension is obtained from the relationship between the volume of the neighbouring film \( (V) \) surrounding the solid and the thickness of the film \( (r) \) (Fig. 3). The resultant equation is:
\[
V\propto\left(\frac{L}{r}\right)^D r^3
\]
where \( L \) is the overall size of the solid and \( D \) the fractal dimension. Another expression to calculate the fractal dimension is from the relation between the surface area and the neighbouring volume film.
\[
\log(A)\propto\frac{(2-D)}{(3-D)}\log(V)
\]
This equation was used by Pfeifer et al. [21] to calculate the roughness of adsorbents by coating them with multiple layers of water. The measurement of the surface area as a function of the number of water layers was carried out with nitrogen by using the BET analysis. We use Eq. (5) to calculate the fractal dimension from the log–log plot of the accessible volume (Eq. (2a)) versus accessible surface area (Eq. (3)) for different solids. The results of the accessible volume, accessible surface area and the fractal dimension for a number of porous solids obtained with different probe molecules are presented in Section 3.
## 3. Simulation and discussion
Having developed a new and consistent methodology based on Monte Carlo (MC) integration to determine the accessible volume and the accessible area, we will illustrate this with a number of model solids such as slit pore, defective slit pore, carbon nanotubes and metal organic frameworks (MDFs). Their structural properties are obtained with noble gases as probe molecules. The fluid solid potential is calculated by using the Lennard Jones (LJ) potential model and fluid–fluid LJ parameters for each gas are shown Table 1.
## 3.1. Graphitic slit pore
Our simplest model solid is a graphitic slit pore whose two parallel walls are made up of graphene layers. The carbon–carbon bond length in the graphene layer is \(0.142\;\mathrm{nm}\) and the LJ carbon–carbon parameters, collision diameter and well depth of interaction energy are \(0.34\:\mathrm{nm}\) and \(28\;\mathrm{K}\), respectively. As an illustration, we choose a physical pore width of \(0.852\;\mathrm{nm}\), which is defined as the distance between the two planes passing through the centres of carbon atoms in the outermost layers of the two pore walls.
Using the MC integration methodology presented in Section 2, we obtain the accessible volume of this pore with various noble gases and the results are shown in Table 2. The data show that the accessible volume decreases with the size of the probe molecule \((\sigma)\). This is as expected because smaller molecules can
approach closer to the solid atoms of the surface. We calculate the surface area from the change of the local accessible volume with distance (Eq. (3)). As it is expected the surface area remains constant with distance for the slit pore for all probe particles. When argon is used, the interfacial solid–fluid area is found to be \(641.36\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), which is slightly larger than the value of \(640.77\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) for a perfectly flat surface. This is because our method can probe the corrugation of the surface and therefore we expect that smaller probe molecules can detect the corrugation better, and indeed this is the case as is shown in Table 2, where we tabulate results for all the noble gases. Small probes give larger accessible volume because they can approach closer to the solid surface, and they also give solid–fluid interfacial areas because they can probe the corrugation better.
We also calculate the fractal dimension for this configuration from the relation between the accessible volume and the surface area (Eq. (6)) and the results are shown in Table 2. The fractal dimensions calculated from all gases are around 2.0, which is close to the expected ideal value for a perfect plane surface.
We have presented the MC results for the accessible volume, accessible area and fractal dimension. The accessible surface area that we have just obtained only gives us the total geometrical measure. Unless all the sites on the surface are the same energetically, this geometrical area does not provide any information about the adsorption affinity. For example, two solid surfaces having the same geometrical surface area do not yield the same adsorption affinity if one surface contains more energetic sites than the other. Therefore, in terms of evaluating the adsorption performance, it is useful to derive information about the fraction of the surface area that is associated with high energy sites. Our method developed above can provide such useful information. To illustrate this we consider three slit pores which total surface area is \(642.56\;\mathrm{nm}^{2}\) and whose walls contains two types of solid atoms. One type of atom has its well depth of interaction ten times greater than that of the other type. The fraction of the weaker solid atoms for each pore is 0.261, 0.52 and 0.738. The derived surface areas for the weaker solid atoms section of the pores are 181.9, 330.0 and \(479.3\;\mathrm{nm}^{2}\), respectively and these are in agreement with the fraction of solid atoms having low well depth.
## 3.2. Graphitic slit pores with defects
We model a defective graphitic slit pore by starting with a graphitic slit whose each of the pore walls consists of two graphene layers separated by a distance of \(0.364\;\mathrm{nm}\) from each other. The two outermost layers (further away from the pore space) are perfect graphene layers while the two inner layers are made defective by selecting a carbon atom at random and then removing it
together with its neighbouring carbon atoms that fall within a specified radius. This process is repeated until a given defect percentage is achieved [24]. In this example, we use a defect radius of \(0.492\,\mathrm{nm}\) and a defect percentage of \(50\%\). The geometrical dimensions of this defective graphitic slit pore are \(H=2.2\,\mathrm{nm}\), \(L_{x}=4.6\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(L_{y}=4.4\,\mathrm{nm}\), where \(H\) is the distance between the two outermost perfect graphene layers and \(L_{x}\) and \(L_{y}\) are the linear dimensions of the graphene layer (see Fig. 4).
From MC integration, we obtain the accessible volume and accessible area for this pore and the results are shown in Table 2 for various noble gases used as the probe molecule. Similar to what we have observed earlier with perfect graphitic slit pores, the accessible volume and area are greater with smaller probes because they can approach closer to the surface. Another feature that arises from this analysis is that the accessible area of a defective pore is greater than that of a perfect graphitic pore because of the presence of defects.
The nature of the defect can be seen by studying the variation of the area with distance from the surface. This is shown in Fig. 5 with argon as the probe molecule. The decrease of the area with distance is an indication of the concavity of the pore, which is attributed to defects on the two innermost layers.
The fractal dimension was calculated by using Eq. (6) and the results are shown in Table 2 for all noble gases. The table shows that the fractal dimension changes according to the probe molecule used. We see that the smallest particle, helium, has a larger fractal dimension than that of xenon because, being a smaller atom, it can probe the irregularities of the surface better.
## 3.3. Single carbon nanotubes
Pores with cylindrical geometry can be studied by taking an example of a single wall carbon nanotube. We choose a physical radius of \(0.4746\,\mathrm{nm}\), which is the distance from the centre of the carbon nanotube to the circle perimeter passing through all the carbon atoms. Our task is to determine the accessible pore volume inside the tube and the accessible area as measured from the inside of the tube and that from the outside of the tube. Using MC integration, we obtain the accessible volume and the results are shown in Table 3 for various noble gases. Like the last two examples, we see that the accessible volume is greater with smaller molecular probe size. The distinct feature that we observe here is that this variation with probe size is more significant than observed earlier with slit pores. This is simply due to the curvature of the pore.
What is really interesting in this example is the results of the accessible area which depend on how it is measured, whether it
is done from the inside of the tube or outside. The results are shown in Table 3. The inner accessible area is greater with smaller probe size. This is due not only to the corrugation of the surface but also to the deeper approach to the carbon atom an consequently greater area. The opposite is observed when we study the outer accessible area. It is greater with larger probe, because larger molecules probe a contour further away from the carbon nanotube and therefore the area is greater due to the curvature of the cylindrical pore.
Next, we show the convexity and concavity of the surface by investigating the variation of the area with distance from the surface. Fig. 6 shows this variation for the inner and outer areas with argon as the molecular probe. The inner area decreases with distance while the outer area increases, suggesting a concave inner pore and a convex outer space outside the pore, which is what we expect.
We computed the fractal dimension for the single wall carbon nanotube by studying the behaviour of the accessible area with respect to the accessible volume. The fractal dimensions for the inside and outside of the nanotube calculated for the different noble gases are shown in Table 3. The fractal dimension for the inside of the nanotube is slightly larger than two while the outside dimension is less than two. The difference is due to the concave–convex shape in the inside and outside of the pore. Additionally, a comparison of the fractal dimension calculated with helium for the slit
pore, the defective pore and the nanotube shows that the nanotube has a more irregular surface than the slit pore but not as fractured as in the case of our model of a defective pore.
## 3.4. Outside the carbon nanotube bundle
Next we study the case of carbon nanotubes arranged triangularly in a bundle as shown in Fig. 7. The physical radius of the nanotubes is \(0.9451\ \mathrm{nm}\). To demonstrate the extent of accessibility of probes with different sizes, we show in this figure the accessible volume of helium. This is shown as a grey region, and it is clear that helium, being a smaller atom, can probe the volume better, especially the interstices between the nanotubes. The variation of area with the distance from the surface is shown for argon in Fig. 8. As expected for the confinement in the interstices, the area decreases rapidly with distance. The accessible volume, surface area and fractal dimension calculated with the different noble gases are presented in Table 4. The accessible volume and the surface area shown in the table are for the enclosed region by the dashed triangle in Fig. 7 that is the representative unit cell to study the order of magnitude of the accessible volume and surface area in the inside of a tube and the interstices of the tube bundle.
We also calculate the fractal dimension of the interstices between nanotubes. Table 5 shows that the fractal dimension is around 2.7 for all the probes used, significantly higher than those for the single nanotube.
## 3.5. Metal organic framework
The last model solid that we use to illustrate our new MC integration method for the determination of the accessible volume and
area is the metal organic frameworks (MOF). The MOFs studied here are MOF-5, 10 and 16. These structures are porous cubic crystals formed by the connection between a \(Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0\) and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate. The main difference between these three MOFs is the ligand lengths, which are 0.5483, 0.9532 and \(1.3581\ \mathrm{nm}\) for MOF-5, MOF-10 and MOF-16, respectively. The three MOF structures are shown in Fig. 9 [25,26]. The LJ parameters for the solid atom in the MOF configurations are taken from Walton and Snurr [26] and they are shown in Table 6.
The accessible pore volume, surface area and fractal dimension for the three MOFs studied are presented in Table 7. The results show a consistent behaviour in the accessible volume with respect
to the molecular size. The table also shows that the volume increases with respect to the MOF size. The accessible volume of
the MOF-10 is almost double compared with the volume of the MOF-5 and an increase around \(86\%\) between MOF-10 and MOF16. In the case of the surface area, the data shows an increase of \(16\%\) between MOF-5 and MOF-10 and around \(36\%\) between MOF5 and MOF-16.
the MOF-10 is almost double compared with the volume of the MOF-5 and an increase around \(86\%\) between MOF-10 and MOF-16. In the case of the surface area, the data shows an increase of \(16\%\) between MOF-5 and MOF-10 and around \(36\%\) between MOF-5 and MOF-16.One interesting feature in the surface area is its variation with respect to the probe molecule size for the three MOFs. That is, for the MOF-5 is seen that the surface area decreases with the size of the probe molecule from 3967.22 to \(3492.59\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) for the results obtained from helium and xenon, respectively. This decrease is about \(13\%\) between the smallest and the largest probe molecules. The results for the MOF-10 show that there is a decrease around \(2.6\%\) for the surface area calculated with the same two probe molecules. In the case of the MOF-16 this trend is reversed and we see an increase about \(2.7\%\) between the area calculated with helium and xenon. The change of the surface area with respect to distance for the MOFs shows also an interesting feature. For the MOF-5 and MOF-10 the surface area decreases with distance while for the MOF-16 its surface area increases. This is because the surface of the MOF is composed by two different structures; one is the corners \((Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0)\) and the other is the organic ligands. As it is expected in the case of corners the surface area decreases with distance as it was seen for concave surfaces, in contrast, the surface area increases with distance for the ligands like in the case of a convex surface. The combined contributions of the corners and ligands give the overall behaviour of the area with respect to distance for the MOF sorbent. For example, in the case of MOF-5 whose ligand length is short, the surface area decreases with distance and this is because of the large contributions of the corners. The opposite is true for the MOF-16 because the greater contribution from the longer ligands (Fig. 10). The fractal dimensions calculated for different probe molecules (Table 7) also confirm this analysis. This is because the fractal dimension of the MOF-5 is around 2.15, which is expected for a concave surface. While in the case of MOF-10 the fractal dimension is around 2.0. This is because of the balance between the contributions of the corners and the
ligands. The fractal dimension for the MOF-16 is around 1.98 because of the dominant contribution of the ligands.
As the MOF structures have four different solid atoms (carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and zinc) we calculate the distribution of the surface area according to each solid atom (Table 8). The figure shows the distribution of the surface area for these MOFs studied
in terms of the type solid atom. As it is expected the results show that as the MOF's ligand length increases the fraction of the area associated with the atoms in the ligand, carbon and hydrogen.
## 4. Conclusions
We have shown a new and unambiguous method based on the Monte Carlo integration to determine the structural parameters of a porous solid: namely accessible pore volume and accessible surface area in a consistent manner. These parameters have been calculated for a number of model solids: slit pores, defective slit pores, carbon nanotubes and metal organic frameworks. The results show that this method can be applied to a wide range of porous solids with known atomistic configuration. We also investigated the distribution of the surface area with respect to the type of solid atoms on the surface. This is useful to study solid configurations with solid atoms having different strength (well depth). We also studied the fractal dimension for different surfaces and we found that a fractal dimension close to two, associated with flat surfaces, is obtained for the concave–convex shapes on the surface even though the surface is not structurally flat, as in the case of MOF.
